  "content": {
    "id": "p32-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "How many ways can you arrange the digits in the number $1234567$ so that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "the first digit is even",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2160$",
          "worked": "There are 3 choices for the first digit: 2, 4 or 6. The remaining six digits can be arranged in $6!$ ways.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=3\\times6!\\\\N&=2160\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the last digit is odd",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2880$",
          "worked": "There are 4 choices for the last digit: 1, 3, 5 or 7. The remaining six digits can be arranged in $6!$ ways.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=4\\times6!\\\\N&=2880\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "the first digit is even and the last digit is odd",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1440$",
          "worked": "There are 3 choices for the first digit and 4 choices for the last digit. These digits are distinct because one is even and the other is odd. The remaining five digits can be arranged in $5!$ ways.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=3\\times4\\times5!\\\\N&=1440\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "the first digit is even or the last digit is odd?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p32-q5-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "3600",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p32-q5-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the counts in (a) and (b), then subtract the overlap counted in (c).",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $A$ be the set of arrangements whose first digit is even and $B$ the set whose last digit is odd. Parts (a)–(c) give $|A|=2160$, $|B|=2880$ and $|A\\cap B|=1440$. By inclusion-exclusion,\n$$\\begin{align*}|A\\cup B|&=|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B|\\\\|A\\cup B|&=2160+2880-1440\\\\|A\\cup B|&=3600.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
